_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nComparing this with the third column of $P,$ we deduce that ${x}_{3}=0.$ Now, setting ${x}_{2}=1$ and substituting our values for ${x}_{2}$ and ${x}_{3}$ into the general solution, we obtain the following: \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFinally, $\\frac{a}{b}=-\\frac{1}{1}=-1.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360744 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186360031 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360744 #edb/ref 1069 "q-97020" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360744 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360744 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 5 & 4 \\\\ 3 & -3 & -3\\end{aligned}],\\,D=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices shown above. Given that $A=PD{P}^{-1},$ which of the following could be the matrix $P?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360744 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "dfd98d7f-725d-5c22-85bf-915bd082de51" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360744 #edb/ref 1074 "Notice that $A=PD{P}^{-1}$ is a diagonalization of the matrix $A.$ So let's apply the diagonalization algorithm to $A.$\n\n**Step 1.** From the diagonal matrix $D,$ we deduce that the eigenvalues of $A$ are \n\n$\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{1}={\\lambda }_{2}=3\\,$ and $\\,{\\lambda }_{3}=-2.$\n\n**Step 2.** Now, we need to find an eigenvector basis of $A.$\n\n- Consider $\\lambda =3.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 5 & 4 \\\\ 3 & -3 & -3\\end{aligned}]-3[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 3 & -3 & -6\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 2 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -3 & -6 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+3{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there is one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ and ${x}_{3}$ are free variables. From the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{2}={x}_{2}+2{x}_{3}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2}+2{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}]={x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{3}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2},{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nAs a result, we have two linearly independent eigenvectors\n\n$\\,$ ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\n- Consider $\\lambda =-2.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-(-2)I & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 5 & 4 \\\\ 3 & -3 & -3\\end{aligned}]-(-2)[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 1 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 7 & 4 \\\\ 3 & -3 & -1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking for nonzero solutions of $(A-(-2)I)x=0,$ we obtain the eigenvector \n\n$\\,$ ${v}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].$\n\n**Step 3.** We construct the invertible matrix $P$ using our eigenvectors, as follows:\n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186404926 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186404927 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186404926 #edb/ref 1069 "q-97007" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186404926 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186404926 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $A$ shown above. Given that $A=PD{P}^{-1},$ which of the following could be matrices $D$ and $P?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186404926 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "2c18b01b-5fe3-525e-a48a-1bfa359e69d8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186404926 #edb/ref 1074 "Notice that $A=PD{P}^{-1}$ is a diagonalization of the matrix $A.$ So let's apply the diagonalization algorithm to $A.$\n\n**Step 1.** We need to find the eigenvalues of $A.$ Since $A$ is a triangular matrix, its eigenvalues are the entries on the main diagonal. Therefore, the eigenvalues are \n\n$\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{1}={\\lambda }_{2}=-3\\,$ and $\\,{\\lambda }_{3}=0.$\n\n**Step 2.** Now, we need to find an eigenvector basis of $A.$\n\n- Consider $\\lambda =-3.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-(-3)I & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0\\end{aligned}]+3[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 3 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{1}↔{R}_{2} \\\\ {R}_{2}↔{R}_{3}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there are two pivot columns (the $1$st and the $2$nd). This implies that we will have $3-2=1$ linearly independent eigenvector corresponding to the current eigenvalue. So, \n\n$$\n\\text{dim}({V}_{-3})=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $A$ is *not* diagonalizable, since \n\n$$\n\\text{dim}({V}_{0})+\\text{dim}({V}_{-3})=1+1=2\\ne 3.\\,\\times \n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186404927 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186404928 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186404927 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186404929 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186404927 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186404930 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186404927 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186404931 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186404927 #